{"reference":"Ecclesiastes 7","book":"Ecclesiastes","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Wisdom without mastery","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Ecclesiastes 7 values wisdom: it can protect and preserve life, and it is set against folly. Yet the chapter also limits what wisdom can accomplish. God’s work remains beyond human control, and neither prosperity nor adversity gives people mastery over what lies ahead. The chapter therefore commends wisdom as a real good without presenting it as a way to predict outcomes or fully discover God’s work.","verse_refs":["Ecclesiastes 7:1-10","Ecclesiastes 7:11-14","Ecclesiastes 7:13-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ecclesiastes_007_aa5a8daa986f030dddaa89d7"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}